# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Counterweight Waits for the Third Strum

**Story & Context:** The opening piece is built around a rule from the first counterweight test: the 808 sub may not enter until Nino completes three full chonguri strum cycles. She begins with a compact Dorian pattern whose final upstroke lands slightly ahead of the expected clap. Mariam answers each cycle with one cable-drum rim hit, making the room itself function as a count.

Darius introduces only kick and clap during the first two cycles. On the third, Lasha enters first with a short bass-trumpet pedal; the 808 sub arrives one beat later, making the electronic weight feel inherited from the brass rather than imposed beneath it.

The middle section repeats the rule at double strum density without changing tempo. The final cycle withholds the sub completely and lets one bass-trumpet fall carry the low register into silence.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Huge funicular counterweight cable beside three chalk strum marks on a maintenance bench, chonguri strings in foreground, 808 drum machine and bass trumpet behind, inspection bell hanging from a steel bracket. No full lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to “three first,” “hold the weight,” “now release,” and “leave the last one acoustic.”

## Track 2: Bell Eight Opens the Brake

**Story & Context:** Mariam assigns the old inspection bell one role only: it may sound after every eighth complete phrase. Darius builds a sparse Miami-bass pattern with long first-cycle 808 tails and progressively shorter ones as the eighth phrase approaches. Nino keeps the same harmony but changes strum emphasis from 3+3+2 to 2+3+3, making the bar feel as though it is tilting uphill.

When the eighth phrase ends, Mariam strikes the bell and Lasha answers with a short half-valve smear. Every drum except clap disappears for one bar. The returning kick enters on the “and” of beat two, creating a deliberate stumble before the groove locks again.

The final bell strike is left unanswered. The track closes with its metal decay crossing one muted chonguri chord.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old brass inspection bell labeled 8 beside a blackened brake wheel, chonguri player mid-upstroke, compact drum machine with several muted steps, bass-trumpet bell reflecting cream enamel signage. Spoken fragments limited to “bell eight,” “open the brake,” “not on one,” and “leave it ringing.”

## Track 3: City Lights Below the Cable Drum

**Story & Context:** The EP closer begins after midnight with the service-house lights off and only Tbilisi visible through the narrow windows. Nino plays the slowest strum pattern on the record, using open strings and long spaces. Darius keeps the tempo unchanged but removes most upper percussion, leaving isolated 808 kicks and soft claps. Lasha uses breath-heavy bass-trumpet entries with almost no full pedal tones.

Halfway through, Mariam rolls the cable drum manually just enough to produce one low mechanical knock and a short metallic creak. The sound is not looped. Instead, the band treats it as a cue to remove sub-bass for sixteen bars. Nino fills the newly open register with stronger body resonance and wider strum voicings.

The 808 returns only once, as a single long low hit beneath the final chonguri chord. The last audible sound is the fading inspection bell and city traffic far below.

